WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.635
This is FAST-LIO2 mapping a city, replayed twenty times faster than real time.

00:00:05.635 --> 00:00:08.826
One Kalman filter, one map, every LiDAR sweep.

00:00:09.206 --> 00:00:13.400
Its authors clock it at thirty-one milliseconds a scan on an Intel laptop chip.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.696
I wanted to know how well it tracks, against ground truth.

00:00:19.877 --> 00:00:20.885
Hi, I'm Tsuru!

00:00:20.885 --> 00:00:24.915
Here is the loop inside FAST-LIO2, and what happened when I ran

00:00:24.915 --> 00:00:26.595
it beside three newer systems.

00:00:28.642 --> 00:00:32.494
Between sweeps, every IMU sample pushes the state forward, and the pose

00:00:32.494 --> 00:00:34.099
after each one is kept.

00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:37.231
Those poses move every point back to where the sensor was at

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:38.369
the end of the sweep.

00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:42.371
Each point finds its five nearest map points, fits a plane, and

00:00:42.371 --> 00:00:44.772
its distance to that plane is the residual.

00:00:45.172 --> 00:00:48.774
The filter solves, re-matches at the new pose, and solves again, until

00:00:48.774 --> 00:00:49.975
the step is tiny.

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:51.813
Then the scan goes into the map.

00:00:54.483 --> 00:00:55.607
Here is the formula.

00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:59.779
Thousands of residual rows go in here, but they are summed

00:00:59.779 --> 00:01:00.977
into one small matrix.

00:01:01.397 --> 00:01:04.179
This is the prior: how sure the IMU prediction was.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.260
So the only inverse is twenty-three by twenty-three, the size of the

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.395
state, however many points the scan had.

00:01:12.158 --> 00:01:14.675
The map lives in an incremental k-d tree.

00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:18.449
New points land on one side, and one branch keeps getting deeper.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.403
Every node on the way back up checks itself.

00:01:21.403 --> 00:01:24.219
This subtree has more than sixty percent on one side.

00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:28.284
So it is flattened into an array and rebuilt balanced from the medians.

00:01:28.284 --> 00:01:30.246
The rest of the tree never moves.

00:01:30.666 --> 00:01:34.013
When the map window slides on, a box delete marks a whole

00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:35.408
subtree dead with one label.

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:38.946
Once more than half of a subtree is dead, the next rebuild

00:01:38.946 --> 00:01:40.246
drops those points for good.

00:01:41.908 --> 00:01:44.952
First, a walk round an Oxford quad.

00:01:44.952 --> 00:01:50.606
FAST-LIO2 tracks to seven point two centimetres over two hundred and eighty-three metres.

00:01:50.606 --> 00:01:51.476
Super-LIO, eight.

00:01:51.856 --> 00:01:53.626
Then a construction-site basement.

00:01:53.626 --> 00:01:56.723
Super-LIO holds at nine point three centimetres.

00:01:56.723 --> 00:02:00.263
FAST-LIO2, with its shipped settings, drifts to fifty.

00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:05.051
With half-metre cubes, the ikd-Tree kept five thousand three hundred points

00:02:05.051 --> 00:02:06.303
for the whole basement.

00:02:06.303 --> 00:02:08.181
Too few to fit planes to.

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:12.278
Super-LIO keeps up to eight points in each voxel, so the same

00:02:12.278 --> 00:02:13.811
room is sampled finely enough.

00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:16.145
Speed.

00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:21.712
Super-LIO took five point six milliseconds a scan to FAST-LIO2's twelve point seven.

00:02:21.712 --> 00:02:27.279
FAR-LIO ran on its CPU build here, so its GPU speed is untested.

00:02:28.342 --> 00:02:29.404
And one switch.

00:02:29.404 --> 00:02:33.652
Skip the deskew loop, and FAST-LIO2's error on the Oxford walk goes

00:02:33.652 --> 00:02:36.838
from seven point two centimetres to thirty point eight.

00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:41.252
Most of these are one filter with different maps.

00:02:41.252 --> 00:02:44.391
Set the map voxel for the space, and keep the deskew.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.351
The filter, the tree, and what four systems did on two real walks.

00:02:51.351 --> 00:02:52.262
Thanks for watching!

00:02:52.682 --> 00:02:54.800
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:55.405
I'm Tsuru.

00:02:55.405 --> 00:02:55.707
Bye!
